==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Akuma CC''' at level 10.

==Strategy/Usage==
Akuma is like a glass-cannon version of [[Shadow Gao (Uber Rare Cat)|Shadow Gao]] with slightly less range. Akuma can be used the same way as Shadow Gao against Traitless enemies, such as [[Mystic Mask Yulala]] or [[R.Ost]]. He is a somewhat viable counter to [[Henry]], with his immunity to freeze and insane damage against him. However, Akuma is usually at the mercy of the supporting enemy choices in this case. Boosting Akuma can be considered a good idea, as at level 50 his maximum damage can reach effective 245,430 DPH and 49,752 DPS. Despite this extreme damage output, there are a few problems that make Akuma somewhat frustrating to use even within his niche. <u>Do not use Akuma for general usage outside of his niche</u>.

Be wary of his less-than-stellar health which reflects poorly with his average 3 KBs, as he won't be able to take much pain compared to Shadow Gao who has 96k health at level 30 for comparison. It's not uncommon for Akuma to take so much damage in a single hit that he skips one of his KBs and dies prematurely. As previously established, Akuma has stellar effective DPS, but this comes in the form of a rapid 3-strike Multi-hit. While many Traitless enemies are somewhat bulky, some enemies such as [[H. Nah]] and [[Rain D.]] are easily knocked back by low damage, resulting in Akuma's damage being cut to as much as 1/3 what it should be. This can at times bring Akuma's effective DPS below what can be provided by similar specialists who are not burdened by split damage.

Akuma's 20 speed makes him quite responsive, but it can also lead him to run head first into danger, especially from backliner enemies that outrange him. He also lacks immunities to abilities such as Wave and Surge which many notable Traitless enemies utilize to snipe backliners. These issues lead into his biggest flaw as a unit: poor survivability. While his DPS is outstanding, his time on the field will often be short-lived due to his very low HP and lack of defensive abilities whatsoever to keep himself active. With a standard Uber cooldown, it's not uncommon for him to be off the field longer than he stays on it despite his otherwise quite safe range. Still, Akuma usually doesn't need a lot of time to do his job and with some support, he can handle most Traitless enemies just fine.

